--TEST--
Every reader `Core\Request` has refuses rather than answering empty in a program that is answering no request -- `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, spec § 15, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`, `rule:routing/a-request-reads-its-mount`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of all fifteen readers: what decides the answer is whether a
// request arrived, and nothing about which member was called. A conformance
// case is a program with no request in front of it -- which is exactly the
// situation `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate` is about, and the same one a scheduled script and a
// job worker are in.
uint $refused = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $verb) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// The verb's second reader is on the same terms as its first: `isHead` reads
// the same token `method` does, so a program with no request has no `false`
// either -- "the peer did not write HEAD" is a fact only a request can carry.
try {
    Core\Request::isHead();
} catch (LogicError $head) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::path();
} catch (LogicError $where) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::query("id");
} catch (LogicError $parameter) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// The three readers of what arrived *with* the request line answer on the same
// terms as the three that are the request line: a header is something a request
// carried, so a program with no request has no empty set of them either.
try {
    Core\Request::header("content-type");
} catch (LogicError $field) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::headers();
} catch (LogicError $fields) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::cookie("session");
} catch (LogicError $crumb) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// The eighth reads what arrived *after* the field block. A body is bytes a
// request carried, so a program with no request has no empty body either --
// and this is the member most exposed to the collapse, since "" is an
// ordinary answer for a request that simply sent nothing.
try {
    Core\Request::body();
} catch (LogicError $raw) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the ninth reads the same bytes the eighth does, a chunk at a time. It is
// the one reader that answers a *walk* rather than a value, so the collapse it
// is exposed to is a different one: an empty `Iterable` reads as a body that
// arrived carrying nothing, and a `foreach` over it would run zero times
// without a program ever learning there was no request. `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`'s two
// ways of reading one body agree here, which is the whole claim.
try {
    Core\Request::bodyStream();
} catch (LogicError $chunks) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the tenth reads those same bytes a third way, as the parts a multipart
// body declares. It is the reader most exposed to the collapse of all: a
// request with no `Content-Type` at all carries no file parts, so an empty walk
// is an *ordinary* answer here in a way it is not for the other two -- which
// is exactly why "there is no request" has to be a different one.
try {
    Core\Request::files();
} catch (LogicError $parts) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the eleventh reads those same bytes a fourth way, as the form the request
// submitted -- `rule:http-server/a-part-is-a-file-iff-it-carries-a-filename`'s non-file parts, or a urlencoded body. It is the
// reader whose empty answer is the most ordinary of all: a `null` for a field
// the form did not carry is what every handler of a form expects to see, so a
// program with no request at all would read one as a peer that left a box
// blank.
try {
    Core\Request::post("title");
} catch (LogicError $form) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the twelfth reads not what the request carried but what the server
// concluded about it before this program started -- `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`'s match. It is
// the only reader here whose ordinary answer is `null` rather than empty:
// nothing matched is a served request like any other, because matching
// dispatches nothing, so a program with no request would read that `null` as a
// peer whose path claimed no route.
try {
    Core\Request::route();
} catch (LogicError $matched) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the thirteenth reads the other thing the server concluded before this
// program started -- `rule:routing/a-request-reads-its-mount`'s mount, the prefix taken off the path and
// the glob captures of the row that took it. Its ordinary answer is the one
// most easily mistaken for an absence: a door that strips nothing answers `""`
// and no captures, so "there is no door at all" cannot be that same pair.
try {
    Core\Request::mount()->prefix();
    Core\Request::mount()->captures();
} catch (LogicError $mounted) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
// And the fourteenth and fifteenth read a bracketed name out of the query and
// out of the form. Their ordinary answer for a name nobody sent is an empty
// array, so a program with no request would read one as a peer that sent no
// values under that name.
try {
    Core\Request::queryArray("tag");
} catch (LogicError $tags) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::postArray("item");
} catch (LogicError $items) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of 15\n";

// The sentence names the member that was read, so a handler that wrapped six
// questions learns which one it was standing on -- and it says why the answer
// is a refusal, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing"
// are the two facts this exists to keep apart.
try {
    Core\Request::path();
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused 15 of 15
Core\Request::path(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
